//
// @IGNORE — DEFECT (both backends, 18594597b): an UNANNOTATED literal whose elements are slices is
//   typed as a collection of iterators.  `vv[0][0] = 66` is refused "Indexing a non vector —
//   keyed collections … have no generic-constructor expression", and `vv.len()` / `"{vv}"` is an
//   internal compiler error, "index out of bounds … [src/scopes/scan.rs:272]".  Annotated
//   (`vv: vector<vector<integer>> = …`) the same literal answers `[[66,50],[10]]`.
//
// A SLICE AS A LITERAL ELEMENT IS A FRESH VECTOR (`@FR-Slice-Value` crossed with `@FR-H-Alloc`).
// "The value is owed at EVERY vector-typed position, not only a bind: … a literal's element
// take[s] the slice as the fresh vector a bind would make of it (`Parser::iterator_as_vector`,
// one home)."  So `[src[3..], src[..1]]` is a `vector<vector<integer>>` of two fresh vectors,
// each its own store (H-Alloc): a write into one reaches neither the other nor the source.
//
// Values hand-computed from `src = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]`.
//
// @falsified-at: none — written against a live defect; it fails on 18594597b on both backends.
// CHANNEL: the compile (a refusal, then an internal compiler error), then the asserts.
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: each message prints the values.
// HOLDS: `a-slice-is-a-fresh-value-at-every-vector-position` beside it.

fn main() {
  src = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];
  vv = [src[3..], src[..1]];
  vv[0][0] = 66;
  assert(vv.len() == 2 && vv[0].len() == 2 && vv[1].len() == 1, "the shape: {vv}");
  assert(vv[0][0] == 66 && vv[0][1] == 50 && vv[1][0] == 10 && src[3] == 40, "the values: {vv} {src}");
}
